<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    THE CHARTER: this project contains exactly the types its contracts speak — nothing else.

    Every type here appears in a contract signature, or in behaviour a contract documents:

      ISpace, the strategy interfaces,
      IRowLandmark/IColumnLandmark                                   the contracts themselves
      Plane                                                          the 2-D locator: the region a
                                                                     projection is handed. Spoken by
                                                                     the strategy signatures and by
                                                                     IProjection's Project
      Point                                                          the 0-D locator Plane mints
      IBound                                                         a region's bottom edge while it
                                                                     is still being discovered; it
                                                                     is public because Plane speaks
                                                                     it, while MINTING one is the
                                                                     engine's business — Plane.Bounded
                                                                     is internal to it
      IValueCells                                                    what a grid of one CLR type
                                                                     answers beyond the canonical
                                                                     four; spoken by the Value read
      Size, Offset, Area                                             the strategy signatures
      OutOfBoundsException                                           the throw ISpace mandates

    Hashes is internal and exempt: the charter is about what this project's contracts SPEAK, and
    nothing outside this package can name it. It exists because the value types here — and the cell
    struct in Unrect.Spreadsheets, which is granted the same reach — mix a hash the same way, and
    System.HashCode is netstandard2.1 and up. Renderings is internal and exempt for the same
    reason: the in-memory grid and the spreadsheet cell must write a double as the same digits,
    and neither target's own rendering agrees with the other's.

    A type that merely USES those contracts belongs above them, in Unrect. That is why GridSpace
    (an implementation of the grid contract) and SpaceExtensions (sugar over one of its methods)
    left on 2026-09-03: both were reachable from here, neither was spoken of by anything here.

    The test before adding a type: name the contract it appears in. If you cannot, it goes in
    Unrect — which ships in the same package, so nothing about the move reaches a consumer but
    the namespace.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;netstandard2.1</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- So the engine can attach a discovered bottom edge to a region it just cut. Reading one is
         public (a plane says whether it carries a bound); making one is not, because only the code
         that owns the scan can honour the contract Plane.Bounded documents. The same grant
         Unrect.Strategies makes for the same kind of reason. The suite gets the same reach, so a
         contract law can be stated over a bounded region without going through the engine. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Unrect" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Unrect.Spreadsheets" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Unrect.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
